    lesson: 'Set this session directly against the last one, because the contrast is the teaching. Forty years earlier twelve men surveyed an entire country across forty days, and the sheer volume of what they brought back could be arranged into two contradictory stories — one of which cost a generation the land. Joshua was one of the two men who reported faithfully. Now Joshua is the one commissioning a survey, and almost every parameter he sets is different. "And Joshua the son of Nun sent out of Shittim two men to spy secretly, saying, Go view the land, even Jericho. And they went, and came into an harlot’s house, named Rahab, and lodged there." (Joshua 2:1) Count the changes. TWO men, not twelve. ONE city — the phrase EVEN JERICHO narrows a whole land to a single objective in two words. SECRETLY, rather than as a delegation. One clear question instead of six. Now draw the principle, because it runs against instinct: a survey can be scoped DOWN, and scoping down is very often the more skilful choice. Breadth produces MATERIAL. Narrowness produces an ANSWER. A man who had watched breadth produce a catastrophe chose narrowness, and the text does not moralise about it — it simply shows him doing it, and the outcome speaks. The mission did not go smoothly, and that matters, because a clean story would teach less. They were detected almost at once: "And it was told the king of Jericho, saying, Behold, there came men in hither to night of the children of Israel to search out the country." (Joshua 2:2) The king sent to the house where they were lodging: "And the king of Jericho sent unto Rahab, saying, Bring forth the men that are come to thee, which are entered into thine house: for they be come to search out all the country." (Joshua 2:3) And the woman who had taken them in concealed them: "But she had brought them up to the roof of the house, and hid them with the stalks of flax, which she had laid in order upon the roof." (Joshua 2:6) Now state the limit plainly, before going further, because this is the place in the course where a teacher is most tempted to supply what Scripture withholds. The Word records what Rahab said to the king’s men. It commends her faith elsewhere. It does not pause to evaluate those words, and this course will not build a doctrine on that silence in either direction — no defence of it and no rebuke of it. We teach what the passage shows and we stop where it stops, which is the same discipline this course held on Leviticus 14 and on Numbers 13, and holding it consistently is what makes it a discipline rather than a convenience. What the passage shows about INSPECTION is what we are here for, and there is a great deal of it. The return has two parts, and collapsing them into one loses half the lesson. FIRST, COMPLETENESS TO THE PRINCIPAL. "So the two men returned, and descended from the mountain, and passed over, and came to Joshua the son of Nun, and told him all things that befell them:" (Joshua 2:23) ALL THINGS THAT BEFELL THEM. Including being spotted. Including being hunted. Including lying flat under stalks of flax on a stranger’s roof while men searched the house. They did not hand their commander a tidy finding and keep the mess to themselves — and that is not modesty, it is essential to the finding’s value. An inspector who reports a conclusion while concealing how difficult it was to reach has given his principal a false confidence in the conclusion. If you could not get onto the far third of the roof, the sentence you owe is not a guess about the far third; it is I could not see this, and here is why. SECOND, THE FINDING ITSELF, and it is four lines: "And they said unto Joshua, Truly the LORD hath delivered into our hands all the land; for even all the inhabitants of the country do faint because of us." (Joshua 2:24) Now ask what is conspicuously absent from it. Jericho’s walls are among the most famous fortifications in the ancient world, and the report does not mention them. The finding is about the PEOPLE — the inhabitants faint — and that is intelligence which simply cannot be gathered from outside a city. It required lodging in a house and listening to how a resident spoke about her own town, and there is no version of that survey done from a distance with a good vantage point. Draw the consequence out, because it generalises hard and it will save somebody a great deal of money: THE MOST DECISIVE THING YOU LEARN ABOUT A PROPERTY IS FREQUENTLY NOT A MEASUREMENT. It is what somebody who has been there thirty years already knows and will tell you if you slow down long enough to ask — about the water, about the road, about the county survey stake at the back of the lot, about what that basement does in March. A tape measure cannot reach it. No photograph contains it. It costs twenty minutes and a willingness to be the person standing at a fence asking a question. Two cautions as this closes. Scoping down is never an excuse for a partial look sold as a whole one. The brief was narrow AND the report was complete, and both halves were required; narrow-and-complete is skill, narrow-and-silent-about-it is the thing lesson four called an evil report. And brevity is not the virtue here — accuracy is. Four lines sufficed because four lines was all they had that mattered, not because short reports are better than long ones. So the work is one question. Before your next look, write down the single question whose answer would actually change your decision — the crawlspace, the panel, the roof plane you cannot see from the street, the neighbour who has been there longest. Go and answer that one properly. Refuse to widen it while you are standing there, because widening it in the moment is how one good answer becomes six loose ones. And when you come back, tell whoever sent you all things that befell you, including the parts you could not reach.',
