        title: 'Painted On A Thursday',
        body: 'The basement was the cleanest basement Oluwaseun had ever stood in, and he said so out loud, twice, and the agent smiled, and he remembers now that the smile was a little bit tired. The floor was swept. The walls were a fresh, even grey. There was nothing stacked against anything. He came out of there having decided the house was cared for, and he had decided it in about ninety seconds on the strength of a swept floor. His cousin, who had worked in the trades for twenty-two years and who came with him on the second visit purely to be polite, walked down the stairs, stood still, and asked when it had been painted. Nobody knew. He ran a thumbnail along the join where the wall met the floor and the paint came away in a strip, soft underneath, and he said, not unkindly, that paint does not dry properly on a damp wall and that this had gone on over something. It had been done nine days earlier. The seller was not a villain and had genuinely meant to make the place look nice, which is what most people mean by it. What Oluwaseun learned, and he says it is the only thing he learned that whole year, is that a clean basement is a fact about the basement’s cleanliness, and he had been treating it as a fact about the house.',
