    lesson: 'This session is the honest ceiling on the whole course, and it has to be taught in both directions at once or it does real damage. Begin with the failure, and notice carefully who commits it. "And it came to pass, when they were come, that he looked on Eliab, and said, Surely the LORD’s anointed is before him." (1 Samuel 16:6) That is Samuel. A prophet. In the middle of a commission he had received directly from Yahweh, sent to a named household for a named purpose — reading a man wrong on sight and being CERTAIN about it. SURELY. Sit with that word. If experience, calling and genuine spiritual authority do not immunise a man against a wrong read from appearance, then nothing you have will either, and that is the first honest thing this session has to say. Then the correction, read whole: "But the LORD said unto Samuel, Look not on his countenance, or on the height of his stature; because I have refused him: for the LORD seeth not as man seeth; for man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart." (1 Samuel 16:7) Now state the limit before any application, as this course has done in every session. This passage is about YAHWEH CHOOSING A KING. It is not a general prohibition on examination. It is not a reason to skip a foundation, a document, a survey, a licence or a reference. And this needs saying out loud, because the verse is deployed constantly to shut down scrutiny — usually by whoever would prefer not to be examined. Doing that INVERTS it. The verse tells us what the eye cannot REACH; it nowhere tells us to close the eye. A course that has spent five sessions teaching people to look carefully cannot now pretend that looking was the problem. The working instruction comes from the Lord Himself, and it is seven words: "Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment." (John 7:24) Read it twice and notice that JUDGE appears in both halves of the sentence. He does not say judge not. He forbids judging BY APPEARANCE and He commands judging RIGHTEOUSLY — which requires more evidence, more time and more work, not less. The whole reconciliation of this session is in that one sentence, and it is His. And Proverbs sets the two postures side by side without needing to comment: "The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going." (Proverbs 14:15) Believing every word is not charity and the proverb does not treat it as a virtue; it calls the man who does it SIMPLE, and it puts LOOKETH WELL TO HIS GOING opposite as the alternative. Now bring it to the practical question, because this is where the session earns its place in a course about buildings. Everything visible is REAL INFORMATION and none of it is SUFFICIENT. And here is the trap, stated precisely: appearance and the thing it stands for are usually CORRELATED, which is exactly what makes appearance so persuasive and so dangerous. A well-kept house usually has been well kept — usually. A freshly painted wall may be a maintained wall or may be a painted wall, and from six feet away in good light those two are indistinguishable. A confident contractor may know his trade or may be confident, and of those two, confidence is much cheaper to acquire. A spotless crawlspace may be a dry crawlspace or a crawlspace cleaned last Thursday by somebody who knew the date of your visit. In every one of those pairs the appearance is a GENUINE FACT. It is simply a fact about the APPEARANCE — and treating it as a fact about the thing is precisely the error Samuel made while holding a prophet’s credentials. So learn the move, and it is mechanical enough to survive a tired evening when you want the decision to be over. First, NAME the conclusion you drew from appearance — say it as a sentence, out loud or on paper, because unspoken conclusions cannot be examined. Second, ask what it would take to actually KNOW it: a document, a measurement, a reference you call, a test, a person who was present. Third, get that — or write down plainly that you have not got it. That third option is honest and it is not defeat. The failure in this whole business is neither looking nor trusting; the failure is HOLDING A CONCLUSION WITH MORE CONFIDENCE THAN ITS EVIDENCE EARNS. That is what Samuel did in one glance, and it is what a man does when he hires somebody because they sounded sure. Close by holding this session against the previous five rather than over them. Leviticus 14 demanded criteria you can see and point at — that IS judging righteous judgment. Nehemiah rode where the beast could not pass — that is refusing to judge by appearance. Numbers 13 asked for answerable pairs and a sample carried home — evidence over impression. Numbers 13:33 is the appearance of the inspector’s own fear handed in as a finding. And Joshua 2 found the decisive thing by sleeping in a house rather than by measuring a famous wall. This session softens none of that. It names the CEILING above all of it — and the right response to a ceiling is not to stop building under it. So write two short lists. Three things you concluded from appearance about the last building or the last person you assessed: how it was presented, how it was kept, how confident somebody sounded, how new something looked. Then, beside each one, what you would need to actually know it. Look hard at the gap between the two lists, because that gap is what your household has been running on. Then pick the single item where being wrong would cost the most, and go close it properly this week.',
