    lesson: 'This session puts the unseen above the visible, and the parable states its principle so economically that a reader can go straight past it. Begin with the builder who gets it right, and slow all the way down on the phrase. "He is like a man which built an house, and digged deep, and laid the foundation on a rock: and when the flood arose, the stream beat vehemently upon that house, and could not shake it: for it was founded upon a rock." (Luke 6:48) DIGGED DEEP. Price that out in your head: time, labour, money, days of a man standing in a hole moving earth. Then ask what it looks like when it is finished. It looks like NOTHING. A deep foundation cannot be photographed. It cannot be shown to a visitor. Nobody has ever walked into a house and admired the footings. Every incentive in a transaction and in a renovation points away from that work, which is precisely why it is the thing to inspect and the thing to pay for. Then the other builder, and read his description carefully, because Luke is more exact than most readers expect: "But he that heareth, and doeth not, is like a man that without a foundation built an house upon the earth; against which the stream did beat vehemently, and immediately it fell; and the ruin of that house was great." (Luke 6:49) He is not called idle. He is not called wicked. He is called WITHOUT A FOUNDATION, and he built a real house that stood on the earth. Now draw the consequence out, because it accounts for very nearly every expensive surprise in this business: A HOUSE WITHOUT A FOUNDATION IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A HOUSE WITH ONE ON A CLEAR DAY. The difference is expressed only under load. Which means it cannot be found by examining the parts that are pleasant to examine, and it will never announce itself at a viewing on a Saturday morning in good weather. Notice as well the word IMMEDIATELY in verse 49. There is no gradual decline here, no warning season, no lengthening list of small symptoms. It stood, and then it fell, and THE RUIN OF THAT HOUSE WAS GREAT. That distinction matters practically: some failures are progressive and forgiving, and this course spent an entire session in Leviticus 14 on the difference between a defect that is spreading and one sitting still. Some failures are not progressive at all. A foundation is in the second category. That is the reason it gets the specialist and the roof usually does not. Then go to Isaiah, where Yahweh describes His own foundation stone and uses a word that belongs in a course on inspection. "Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I lay in Zion for a foundation a stone, a tried stone, a precious corner stone, a sure foundation: he that believeth shall not make haste." (Isaiah 28:16) A TRIED STONE. Tried means tested — and notice the ORDER, because the order is the whole thing: it was tried BEFORE it was laid. It was not discovered to be sound after a building was already standing on top of it. That is the entire logic of inspection compressed into two words, and it is Yahweh’s own practice with His own cornerstone. And the clause that ends the verse deserves its own moment: HE THAT BELIEVETH SHALL NOT MAKE HASTE. A man standing on something tried does not have to hurry — and hurry is the single most reliable signal in any transaction that somebody would prefer you did not look too closely. When you are being rushed, that is data. Then the instruments: "Judgment also will I lay to the line, and righteousness to the plummet: and the hail shall sweep away the refuge of lies, and the waters shall overflow the hiding place." (Isaiah 28:17) A LINE and a PLUMMET. A measuring line, and a weight on a cord that shows true vertical. Both are indifferent to opinion; neither can be talked round or charmed. And it is worth noticing that Yahweh reaches for the vocabulary of MEASUREMENT when He speaks about judgment and righteousness — which says something about how He regards measurement, and anybody who has been taught to treat scrutiny as unspiritual should sit with that. Then see what the hail does: it sweeps away THE REFUGE OF LIES, and the waters overflow THE HIDING PLACE. Whatever has been covering something up is removed by weather in the end. The only question that was ever open is whether YOU find it, or the storm does. Hold the limit as this closes, because it matters and the passage states it itself. The primary subject of this parable is not masonry. Verse 47 is explicit — the man who hears His sayings AND DOES THEM is the one who digged deep — and the verse just before it puts the question harder still: "And why call ye me, Lord, Lord, and do not the things which I say?" (Luke 6:46) The house is His illustration, not His topic, and a teacher who reduces this to construction advice has taken the smaller half and left the greater one on the table. But the illustration is chosen, and it is exact, and the same Lord who told it also said to judge righteous judgment. Both halves stand together. So make two lists of five, and let the exercise cost you something. First, the five things about the place you are looking at that you can see from the street or in the photographs. Second, the five that actually decide whether it stands. Look at how little those lists overlap. Then spend your money and your attention in the order of the SECOND list, which will feel wrong, because the first list is what you look at every day and what visitors comment on. And if your household can afford exactly one specialist — which is the real situation for most people — do not spend that one on anything already visible to your own eyes. Being worried about something is not the same as not knowing it. Spend him under the house, or on the structure, the water, or the electrical: the things that are expensive to get wrong and impossible to judge from a doorway. Then write down which one you chose and why, so that next time you can look back and see whether you chose well, instead of choosing the same way again without ever checking.',
